The pattern/name split is purely syntactic, which leaves two soft spots.
A bare prefix like 'wip/' is classified as a pattern, so it reaches
for-each-ref and (as before this change) matches nothing rather than
erroring -- we don't try to make 'wip/' mean "every wip branch". And a
branch whose name literally contains *, ?, or [ would be misread as a
glob; that seems an acceptable price for not forking per argument.

One more edge: --symbolic-full-name prints a blank line for an argument
that resolves to a non-symbolic object (a raw SHA), so we filter empty
lines out of its output. That means a SHA passed as a "name" is dropped
silently rather than erroring -- but feeding a SHA to a tool that nests
branches is nonsensical, so I didn't guard it further.

